# Error Handling

## Typed error hierarchy

```typescript
// src/lib/errors.ts
AppError
├── ValidationError    // 400 — invalid input
├── UnauthorizedError  // 401 — not authenticated
├── ForbiddenError     // 403 — not authorized
├── NotFoundError      // 404 — resource missing
└── ConflictError      // 409 — duplicate/conflict
```

## Service layer

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD — typed errors
if (!beat) throw new NotFoundError("Beat not found");
if (beat.producerId !== userId) throw new ForbiddenError();

// ❌ BAD — generic errors
throw new Error("Something went wrong");
```

## API layer

Always `formatErrorResponse(error)` in catch — consistent JSON shape.

## Client layer

- `ApiError` in `@/lib/api/http.ts` — parses `{ error }` from failed responses.
- Display user-friendly messages — never show raw error objects or stack traces.
- Provide retry actions for transient failures (network, timeout).

## UI error boundaries

- Route-level `error.tsx` for page crashes.
- `global-error.tsx` for root layout errors.
- Inline error states for form submissions and data fetching.

## Defensive data rendering

Never trust that database fields are populated — always guard nullable values in templates, even if the schema says `required`. Legacy data, partial updates, and race conditions can leave fields `undefined`.

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD — guard with nullish coalescing
<p>₹{amount ?? 0}</p>
<p>{title ?? "—"}</p>
<p>{count != null ? `${count} items` : "—"}</p>
<time>{date ? formatDate(date) : "—"}</time>

// ❌ BAD — raw interpolation of potentially undefined values
<p>₹{amount}</p>           // renders "₹undefined"
<p>{title}</p>             // renders "undefined"
<p>{count} items</p>       // renders "undefined items"
```

Rules:
- Always use `?? fallback` or `!= null` checks before interpolating values in JSX.
- Currency values: `₹${value?.toLocaleString("en-IN") ?? "0"}`.
- Dates: check truthiness before calling `new Date(...)` or `toLocaleDateString()`.
- Prefer `"—"` (em dash) as the fallback for missing display values, not empty strings.

## Every feature must have

- [ ] User-friendly error messages
- [ ] Retry strategy for transient failures
- [ ] Structured logging of errors
- [ ] Fallback UI (not blank screen)
- [ ] Defensive rendering of nullable data values
